answer.text:
Assuming \(M_1\) is an n-channel enhancement MOSFET, with body tied to source, and neglecting channel-length modulation:

- \(V_{GS}=V_X\)
- \(V_{DS}=1\text{ V}\) (fixed by the 1 V source)
- \(I_X\) is positive in the arrow direction (current into the drain).

Then
\[
I_X=
\begin{cases}
0, & V_X\le V_T\\[4pt]
\dfrac12 k_n (V_X-V_T)^2, & V_T<V_X\le V_T+1\\[6pt]
k_n\left[(V_X-V_T)-\dfrac12\right], & V_X> V_T+1
\end{cases}
\]
where \(k_n=\mu_n C_{ox}W/L\).

So the sketch is:

- zero for \(V_X\le V_T\),
- a smooth square-law rise for \(V_T<V_X<V_T+1\),
- then a straight line of constant slope \(k_n\) for \(V_X>V_T+1\).

The curve is continuous and has the same slope at the transition point:
\[
V_X=V_T+1,\qquad I_X=\frac{k_n}{2}.
\]

Qualitative sketch:

\[
\begin{array}{c}
I_X\\[2pt]
\uparrow\\
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(6,0) node[right]{\(V_X\)};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,4) node[above]{\(I_X\)};
\draw[thick] (0.8,0)--(2.3,0);
\draw[thick] plot[smooth,domain=2.3:4.0] (\x,{0.5*(\x-2.3)^2});
\draw[thick] (4.0,{0.5*(4.0-2.3)^2})--(5.6,{0.5*(4.0-2.3)^2+1.0*(5.6-4.0)});
\fill (2.3,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below]{\(V_T\)};
\fill (4.0,{0.5*(4.0-2.3)^2}) circle (1.5pt);
\node at (4.0,-0.35){\(V_T+1\)};
\node at (1.3,0.2){0};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{array}
\]

In words: no current until the threshold, then the current increases ever more steeply in saturation, and once \(V_{GS}-V_T>V_{DS}=1\text{ V}\) the MOSFET enters the triode/linear region, giving a straight-line increase in \(I_X\) with \(V_X\). There is no flat plateau when \(V_X\) is increased with \(V_{DS}\) held fixed.

If a common value \(V_T=1\text{ V}\) is intended, the curve starts at \(V_X=1\text{ V}\) and changes from square-law to linear at \(V_X=2\text{ V}\). For a p-channel device, use the complementary/mirrored characteristic.
